---
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// THE LATHE — flagship case study.
//
// A scroll-driven film in six acts. We follow one anonymous file —
// Audio 14.wav — from a lost glint in the chaos to a named, crowned,
// published master. The system is what does the transforming.
//
//   0 · Cold open      — the title card (AtlasHero)
//   1 · Chaos          — make the mess felt (ChaosToBuckets)
//   2 · The sweep      — one organizing motion into five bins
//   3 · The companion  — zoom to one track; Richard appears (RichardReveal)
//   4 · The brain      — taste → 6 variants → the rendition toggle → the loops
//   5 · Resolution     — what it solves, the scale, the meta-twist
//
// Supersedes the prose case study in place (route /case-studies/the-console,
// wired to the homepage card + cover). Backup: the-console.astro.bak-pre-atlas-*.
// Honors prefers-reduced-motion; all motion is IntersectionObserver-gated.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
import CSLayout from "../../components/case-study/CSLayout.astro";
import CSSection from "../../components/case-study/CSSection.astro";
import CSPullquote from "../../components/case-study/CSPullquote.astro";
import CSStatGrid from "../../components/case-study/CSStatGrid.astro";
import CSLiveDemo from "../../components/case-study/CSLiveDemo.astro";
import AtlasHero from "../../components/case-study/atlas/AtlasHero.astro";
import ChaosToBuckets from "../../components/case-study/atlas/ChaosToBuckets.astro";
import RichardReveal from "../../components/case-study/atlas/RichardReveal.astro";
import EngineerBrain from "../../components/case-study/atlas/EngineerBrain.astro";
import RenditionToggle from "../../components/case-study/atlas/RenditionToggle.astro";
import TasteLoops from "../../components/case-study/atlas/TasteLoops.astro";

export const prerender = true;
---

<CSLayout
  title="The Lathe — Jon Tollefson"
  description="The Lathe — eight years of scattered sound, swept into one place that knows what it has and hands it back to you, smarter. A live, interactive case study with a mix engineer that learns your taste."
  ogImage="/case-studies/the-console/cover.jpg"
>
  <AtlasHero
    eyebrow="Case study 01 · A private AI surface hiding inside this portfolio"
    title="The Lathe"
    subtitle="Eight years of scattered sound. One place that knows what it has — and hands it back to you, smarter."
    meta={[
      { label: "Role", value: "Designer, builder, sole engineer" },
      { label: "Timeline", value: "May 2026 — present" },
      { label: "Stack", value: "Astro · FastAPI · Python · Pedalboard · Whisper · Claude" },
      { label: "Scale", value: "20,279 files · 5 tiers · 6 mixes a track" },
    ]}
    glossary={{
      term: "lathe",
      ipa: "/leɪð/",
      pos: "noun",
      senses: [
        {
          tag: "machining",
          text: "A machine tool that grips a workpiece and spins it about a single axis while a fixed cutting edge is fed against it, paring rough stock down to a precise, symmetrical form — the foundational machine of the workshop.",
        },
        {
          tag: "audio",
          text: "The cutting lathe that masters a record: a heated stylus inscribes the audio signal as one continuous spiral groove into a lacquer — the master every pressing is struck from.",
        },
      ],
      etymology: "Middle English, of Scandinavian origin — a stand or frame that holds the work and turns it.",
    }}
  />

  {/* ─────── 01 · THE WISH / THE MESS ─────── */}
  <CSSection
    num="01"
    title="Sit down to make something"
    deck="The blank session waits. You half-remember a vocal you sang into your phone last Tuesday. You open Finder. iCloud hasn't downloaded the file yet. By the time it shows up, the idea is gone."
    noTopRule
  >
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>That moment — repeated a thousand times across eight years of writing music — is the gap this whole thing closes. Twenty thousand audio files: every voice memo, every jam, every bounce, every sample I've ever touched, scattered across four drives and iCloud and my desktop and friends' DMs. None of it findable. Most of it never heard again.</p>
      <p>So I built a machine to sweep it all into one place. Scroll.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── ACT 1 + 2 · CHAOS → THE SWEEP (theater) ─────── */}
  <ChaosToBuckets />

  {/* ─────── 02 · THE CATALOG KNOWS WHAT IT HAS ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="02" title="One inventory, sorted by what things are" deck="AudioDex fills in its own entries: every file is read, classified, and filed the moment it lands.">
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>An auto-classifier reads every row's content, source, and length and drops it into a tier. <strong>Jams</strong> (long musical captures) and <strong>Field</strong> (voice memos plus audio pulled out of videos) sort themselves — the first run bucketed 1,389 field rows and 329 jams without me touching anything. <strong>Loops</strong> and <strong>Tracks</strong> stay curator-picked. <strong>Inbox</strong> is the residue I haven't gotten to. Five tiers, and the view becomes the index.</p>
      <p>Then the files start <em>speaking</em>. Whisper transcribes the voice memos; Claude turns a ninety-minute ramble into one legible sentence. A jam I'd forgotten surfaced because the engine noticed two sixteen-bar sections worth pulling. Librosa stamps BPM and key on every audio row at ingest, so every tool downstream gets to assume them. The catalog stops being a pile of filenames and becomes a thing that knows what it's holding — and our one file, <code>Audio 14.wav</code>, just landed in Tracks.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── ACT 3 · THE COMPANION ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="03" title="If only I had an engineer" deck="Zoom all the way in on one row in Tracks. It's a finished song — mixed rough, on headphones, at 2 a.m. The next step has always been the lonely one: master it, A/B it against records you love, decide if it's done. So I built someone to do it with.">
    <RichardReveal />
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── ACT 4a · THE TASTE ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="04" title="An engineer with taste" deck="Richard isn't a preset — he's a guitar-slinging turtle with opinions. His taste is built from a corpus of finished records I love — each one tagged with what actually works in it. Those notes anchor three axes, and every job masters the track six ways across them.">
    <CSLiveDemo label="LIVE · TAP A RECORD" caption="The real reference catalog — loved artists plus a one-line note on what I like in each. Tap a record to see which taste pole it anchors. matchering matches the track's tonal balance and loudness to a reference tagged for that pole, then holds a −1 dBTP ceiling. Six variants come back per song." bleed pad="comfy">
      <EngineerBrain />
    </CSLiveDemo>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── ACT 4b · THE RENDITION TOGGLE (centerpiece) ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="05" title="The same eight seconds, three ways" deck="This is the whole feeling. Six takes come back; here are three of them on one track. Press 1 · 2 · 3 — the mix changes underneath you, but the playhead never loses its place. You're always hearing the same moment, just engineered differently.">
    <CSLiveDemo
      label="LIVE · PRESS 1 · 2 · 3"
      caption="Real audio, synthesized in your browser: one musical loop, fanned through three loudness-matched masters — forward & bright, warm & recessed, punchy & open. Switching crossfades which one you hear without moving the clock, so it's an honest A/B. Blind by default; hit reveal to see each take's identity and the record it was mastered toward. Hold a key to crown one. This is the gesture the whole Engineer is built around."
      bleed
      pad="comfy"
    >
      <RenditionToggle />
    </CSLiveDemo>
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>In the real app, those three numbered takes are a blind A/B — the original is shuffled in unlabelled, every clip plays at a matched level, and you hold a key to vote. No pole names, no "this one's the master" tell. You're voting on the sound, and the engine is the only one keeping score. Three axes judged, three winners, and it consolidates them into one card per song.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── ACT 4c · THE LOOPS ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="06" title="It learns — and then it asks">
    <TasteLoops />
    <div class="prose-block" style="margin-top: 34px;">
      <p>The asking is the part I didn't expect to build. The engine scans its own vote history for things that <em>surprise</em> it — a track where the original beat every master, a QA-flagged take you crowned anyway, the same complaint three times — and turns the most surprising one into a question, in its own voice, citing the real tally. Your answer doesn't retrain anything wholesale; it becomes a small, bounded nudge on which references it reaches for next time, scoped to songs that sound like this one. The taste loop runs both directions now.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── ACT 5 · RESOLUTION ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="07" title="What it actually solves" deck="Audio 14.wav is a crowned, published master now. It has a name. The path from “this loop is interesting” to “this track is out” no longer crosses a single tool boundary — and the machine never once made a creative choice for me.">
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>Eight years of scattered output became AudioDex — one inventory that knows what it has and surfaces what's worth hearing by objective signal, not vibes the computer can't judge. From there a finished track goes to Richard, the engineer that learns my taste: A/B'd blind, crowned, and pushed to a public page, all from the same row, in the same database, with the same verbs. The friction that used to sit between an idea and a finished thing is mostly gone.</p>
      <p>AudioDex to know what I have, Richard to hand it back smarter — together, the Lathe.</p>
    </div>

    <CSStatGrid
      stats={[
        { n: 20279, label: "files in one catalog", color: "white" },
        { n: 1718, label: "auto-sorted, untouched", color: "cyan" },
        { n: 6, label: "masters per track", color: "pink" },
        { n: 7, suffix: "%", label: "original-win, down from 67", color: "green" },
      ]}
    />

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>And here's the twist you may have already felt: this case study is a window onto a system that's otherwise private. The Lathe lives behind a login at <code>uxjon.com/portal</code> — a portfolio site that quietly doubles as my own AI surface. There's a door on the home page, too. Type the word <code>cloud</code> anywhere and watch what happens. The thing that hands my creative output back to me, smarter, has been one keystroke away this whole time.</p>
    </div>

    <ul class="wont">
      <li>It won't write my posts, propose albums, or pitch song concepts.</li>
      <li>It won't push daily prompts or listen during a session.</li>
      <li>It won't publish to streaming, or touch the desktop.</li>
      <li>It won't make a single creative decision for me.</li>
    </ul>

    <CSPullquote hero cite="The Lathe">
      Utility, not synthesis. The machine sorts, fingerprints, surfaces, and removes friction. The artist still chooses, still drags, still arranges.
    </CSPullquote>
  </CSSection>

  <div class="cs-foot">
    <a href="/#work" class="cs-foot-back">← back to selected work</a>
  </div>
</CSLayout>

<style>
  .prose-block { max-width: 64ch; }
  .prose-block em { font-style: italic; color: var(--cs-text); }

  .wont {
    list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 26px 0 8px; max-width: 64ch;
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 6px 22px;
  }
  @media (max-width: 720px) { .wont { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
  .wont li {
    position: relative; padding-left: 22px;
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-size: 13px; color: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.78); line-height: 1.5;
  }
  .wont li::before { content: "·"; position: absolute; left: 6px; color: var(--brand-pink); font-weight: 700; }

  .cs-foot { text-align: center; padding: 64px 0 0; }
  .cs-foot-back {
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.22em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--brand-orange);
    text-decoration: none;
    padding: 8px 16px;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 90, 60, 0.32);
    border-radius: 2px;
    transition: color 160ms ease, border-color 160ms ease, background 160ms ease;
  }
  .cs-foot-back:hover {
    color: #f5f1e8;
    border-color: var(--brand-orange);
    background: rgba(255, 90, 60, 0.08);
  }
</style>
